WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.106 --> 00:00:07.917
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.918 --> 00:00:09.736
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.737 --> 00:00:12.624
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:14.467
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:17.165
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.178 --> 00:00:19.231
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.231 --> 00:00:20.948
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:24.883
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are going

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:27.383
to be talking about the British Museum.

00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:30.540
It's a story about a doctor with

00:00:30.544 --> 00:00:32.730
a collecting problem, the British

00:00:32.749 --> 00:00:35.301
Empire, some stones the Greeks want

00:00:35.338 --> 00:00:37.712
back, and the unanswered question

00:00:37.946 --> 00:00:40.896
of who the past actually belongs to.

00:00:41.594 --> 00:00:43.582
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:43.588 --> 00:00:46.151
minute and get right into it.

00:00:48.753 --> 00:00:50.890
If you have been to London,

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:53.211
there is a good chance you have

00:00:53.216 --> 00:00:55.187
visited the British Museum.

00:00:56.169 --> 00:00:58.998
In total, its collection numbers

00:00:59.043 --> 00:01:01.568
around eight million objects.

00:01:02.009 --> 00:01:04.354
They aren't all on display; on any

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:07.452
given day, you can see perhaps 80,000

00:01:07.452 --> 00:01:10.042
of them, a mere 1 per cent of the total.

00:01:11.122 --> 00:01:12.661
That is still a lot.

00:01:13.249 --> 00:01:16.164
To give you an idea of quite how much, if

00:01:16.165 --> 00:01:18.933
you wanted to see each object on display,

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.361
and you spent only one minute with each

00:01:22.364 --> 00:01:25.859
one, it would take you almost two months.

00:01:26.106 --> 00:01:27.817
That is, if you didn't stop to

00:01:27.818 --> 00:01:30.570
sleep, or eat, and the museum allowed

00:01:30.571 --> 00:01:32.104
you to stay throughout the night.

00:01:32.828 --> 00:01:35.144
If you did the same for the entire

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.570
collection, the full eight million,

00:01:37.908 --> 00:01:39.914
you would finish your visit a

00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:42.628
little over fifteen years from now.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.963
There is a vast amount of stuff:

00:01:46.352 --> 00:01:48.828
sculptures from Athens, mummies from

00:01:48.863 --> 00:01:52.785
Egypt, bronzes from Nigeria, chess pieces

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.737
from a Scottish beach, and of course a

00:01:55.753 --> 00:01:58.396
stone from the banks of the Nile that

00:01:58.444 --> 00:02:00.921
unlocked the secrets of ancient Egypt.

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:04.934
So the obvious question is: how did all

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:08.270
of this end up in one building in London?

00:02:09.543 --> 00:02:11.041
The answer begins with

00:02:11.041 --> 00:02:13.425
a man named Hans Sloane.

00:02:14.688 --> 00:02:17.582
Sloane was born in Ireland in

00:02:17.642 --> 00:02:21.078
1660, and after training as a

00:02:21.095 --> 00:02:23.225
physician, he became one of the

00:02:23.225 --> 00:02:25.371
most fashionable doctors in London.

00:02:26.439 --> 00:02:29.031
He treated three monarchs, became

00:02:29.057 --> 00:02:31.160
president of the Royal Society,

00:02:31.586 --> 00:02:34.283
and even has a square in Chelsea

00:02:34.546 --> 00:02:37.013
named after him: Sloane Square.

00:02:38.376 --> 00:02:41.016
As a young man, he spent over a

00:02:41.016 --> 00:02:43.829
year in Jamaica as a doctor to the

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.485
island's governor, and he came home

00:02:46.714 --> 00:02:49.699
with around 800 plant specimens.

00:02:50.427 --> 00:02:53.390
See, Sloane's passion was collecting.

00:02:53.887 --> 00:02:55.777
He collected plants, animals,

00:02:56.013 --> 00:02:58.854
coins, medals, books, manuscripts,

00:02:58.926 --> 00:03:01.293
shoes, drawings, and what he called

00:03:01.452 --> 00:03:04.215
"curiosities" from all over the world.

00:03:05.144 --> 00:03:07.154
Today, he might be called a

00:03:07.358 --> 00:03:09.357
"hoarder", someone who keeps

00:03:09.411 --> 00:03:11.857
everything and throws nothing away.

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:17.164
By the time he died in 1753, aged

00:03:17.212 --> 00:03:20.520
92, his collection had swollen

00:03:20.585 --> 00:03:23.932
to more than 71,000 objects.

00:03:25.282 --> 00:03:28.053
In his will, he offered his entire

00:03:28.097 --> 00:03:30.106
collection to the nation, on the

00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:32.225
condition that Parliament pay

00:03:32.227 --> 00:03:35.166
his daughters £20,000, which is

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.530
almost £4 million in today's money.

00:03:39.361 --> 00:03:41.956
A lot of money, of course, but it was a

00:03:41.972 --> 00:03:44.856
fraction of what the collection was worth.

00:03:46.105 --> 00:03:48.468
He also asked that the collection be

00:03:48.469 --> 00:03:51.373
kept together, and shown to anyone

00:03:51.375 --> 00:03:53.739
who was curious, free of charge.

00:03:55.023 --> 00:03:57.423
Parliament agreed, and launched

00:03:57.586 --> 00:03:59.555
a national lottery to pay for it.

00:04:00.795 --> 00:04:03.723
Once the funds were raised, the collection

00:04:03.787 --> 00:04:06.044
passed into the hands of the state, and

00:04:06.051 --> 00:04:08.493
a building was bought to house it all.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.764
And so in 1759, in a mansion called

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:16.364
Montagu House in Bloomsbury, the

00:04:16.373 --> 00:04:18.563
British Museum opened its doors.

00:04:19.288 --> 00:04:22.027
It was the first national museum in the

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:25.558
world that belonged to neither a king nor

00:04:25.559 --> 00:04:28.718
a church, open to what its founding rules

00:04:28.718 --> 00:04:32.400
called "all studious and curious persons".

00:04:33.081 --> 00:04:35.354
Curious minds, one might even say.

00:04:36.694 --> 00:04:39.245
In the early years, visiting

00:04:39.271 --> 00:04:40.961
was not particularly easy.

00:04:41.487 --> 00:04:44.326
You had to apply in writing for a ticket,

00:04:44.571 --> 00:04:47.058
wait weeks, and then be marched through

00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:49.757
the rooms in a small group by an official.

00:04:50.579 --> 00:04:53.240
But from the start, the collection

00:04:53.508 --> 00:04:55.655
belonged to the public, and

00:04:55.883 --> 00:04:58.199
that was a bold new idea.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.975
And the collection kept growing.

00:05:02.833 --> 00:05:05.954
Now that Britain had a national museum,

00:05:06.218 --> 00:05:08.465
there was somewhere for things to go.

00:05:09.425 --> 00:05:11.787
Wealthy collectors followed Sloane's

00:05:11.788 --> 00:05:14.649
example and left their collections to it.

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:17.850
Parliament bought others, including

00:05:17.856 --> 00:05:20.662
hundreds of ancient Greek vases collected

00:05:20.663 --> 00:05:22.871
by the British ambassador in Naples.

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:26.866
As the empire expanded, and British

00:05:26.884 --> 00:05:29.042
ships sailed further and further from

00:05:29.053 --> 00:05:31.764
home, their crews brought back objects

00:05:31.768 --> 00:05:33.821
from all corners of the globe, and

00:05:34.053 --> 00:05:36.341
many of them ended up in the museum.

00:05:37.339 --> 00:05:40.499
And by law, the museum received a copy

00:05:40.512 --> 00:05:42.596
of every book published in Britain.

00:05:43.906 --> 00:05:46.493
Within a few decades, Montagu

00:05:46.506 --> 00:05:48.845
House was bursting at the seams.

00:05:48.965 --> 00:05:50.514
It was completely full.

00:05:51.563 --> 00:05:53.920
In the 19th century, it was knocked

00:05:53.958 --> 00:05:56.684
down and replaced by the great columned

00:05:56.711 --> 00:05:58.805
building you might have visited yourself.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.723
Soon enough, this building also started

00:06:02.723 --> 00:06:05.265
to fill up, but instead of knocking

00:06:05.266 --> 00:06:07.123
it down and building a bigger one,

00:06:07.462 --> 00:06:09.919
the solution was to move parts of the

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.286
collection to other museums in London.

00:06:13.274 --> 00:06:15.978
Sloane's dried plants, stuffed animals

00:06:16.151 --> 00:06:18.606
and pickled creatures were moved out

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:21.965
to South Kensington, where in 1881

00:06:22.209 --> 00:06:24.295
they became the Natural History Museum.

00:06:25.004 --> 00:06:26.854
The books and manuscripts, as

00:06:26.856 --> 00:06:29.041
we will see, would leave later.

00:06:29.817 --> 00:06:32.278
What stayed in Bloomsbury were

00:06:32.303 --> 00:06:34.377
the things made by people, things

00:06:34.517 --> 00:06:36.734
from often distant civilisations.

00:06:37.536 --> 00:06:39.648
And many of these arrived in

00:06:39.648 --> 00:06:41.967
London thanks to British military

00:06:42.119 --> 00:06:43.769
and political power abroad.

00:06:44.829 --> 00:06:48.214
One such example is the museum's single

00:06:48.228 --> 00:06:51.317
most famous object: the Rosetta Stone.

00:06:52.151 --> 00:06:53.678
Now, we've made a full

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:55.399
episode on the Rosetta Stone.

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:58.644
It's episode number 141, so

00:06:58.844 --> 00:07:00.485
if you'd like the full story,

00:07:00.715 --> 00:07:01.985
you can listen to it there.

00:07:02.652 --> 00:07:06.483
But as a brief summary, or as a reminder,

00:07:06.929 --> 00:07:11.050
in 1798, Napoleon invaded Egypt.

00:07:11.969 --> 00:07:14.346
The following year, French soldiers

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:16.876
rebuilding a fort near the town

00:07:16.898 --> 00:07:20.264
of Rosetta dug up a broken slab of

00:07:20.293 --> 00:07:23.336
dark stone, covered in three bands

00:07:23.337 --> 00:07:26.239
of writing: ancient Greek, everyday

00:07:26.419 --> 00:07:29.191
Egyptian script, and hieroglyphics.

00:07:30.397 --> 00:07:32.123
The scholars Napoleon had

00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:33.987
brought with him understood

00:07:34.067 --> 00:07:36.137
immediately what this could mean.

00:07:37.343 --> 00:07:39.551
Nobody on Earth had been able

00:07:39.551 --> 00:07:41.619
to read hieroglyphics for

00:07:41.619 --> 00:07:43.532
well over a thousand years.

00:07:44.256 --> 00:07:47.476
If all three texts said the same

00:07:47.487 --> 00:07:50.446
thing, the Greek could be used as

00:07:50.447 --> 00:07:53.222
a key, a dictionary to translate

00:07:53.396 --> 00:07:55.223
this ancient Egyptian script.

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:58.745
But the French, alas, never

00:07:58.746 --> 00:08:00.179
got to keep their prize.

00:08:00.848 --> 00:08:03.387
In 1801, the British defeated

00:08:03.425 --> 00:08:05.581
Napoleon's forces in Egypt, and

00:08:05.886 --> 00:08:07.469
under the terms of the French

00:08:07.476 --> 00:08:10.063
surrender, the stone was handed over.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:13.546
It has been in the British Museum

00:08:13.818 --> 00:08:17.209
since 1802, and it remains the single

00:08:17.367 --> 00:08:19.530
most visited object in the building.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.047
And it's closely followed

00:08:22.255 --> 00:08:24.000
by the Parthenon Marbles.

00:08:25.386 --> 00:08:28.387
At the start of the 19th century, Greece

00:08:28.565 --> 00:08:31.573
was ruled by the Ottoman Empire, and the

00:08:31.589 --> 00:08:34.151
British ambassador to the Ottomans was

00:08:34.152 --> 00:08:37.230
a Scottish aristocrat called Lord Elgin.

00:08:38.703 --> 00:08:43.153
Between 1801 and 1812, Elgin's

00:08:43.247 --> 00:08:46.018
agents removed about half of the

00:08:46.031 --> 00:08:47.963
surviving sculptures from the

00:08:47.993 --> 00:08:50.051
Parthenon, the ancient temple in

00:08:50.092 --> 00:08:52.858
Athens, and shipped them to Britain.

00:08:54.519 --> 00:08:57.104
Elgin claimed he had permission from

00:08:57.105 --> 00:08:59.244
the Ottoman authorities, although

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.271
the original document giving him

00:09:01.274 --> 00:09:03.206
permission has never been found.

00:09:03.923 --> 00:09:06.475
The Greeks were never consulted, and

00:09:06.573 --> 00:09:08.813
if they had been, it seems extremely

00:09:08.856 --> 00:09:10.685
unlikely they would have agreed.

00:09:11.851 --> 00:09:14.513
Now, not to suggest that Elgin is

00:09:14.533 --> 00:09:16.560
deserving of a huge amount of sympathy,

00:09:16.895 --> 00:09:20.372
but the logistics of removing the marbles

00:09:20.571 --> 00:09:23.760
proved far more complicated and expensive

00:09:24.029 --> 00:09:26.357
than the ambassador had bargained for.

00:09:27.566 --> 00:09:30.413
By the time the marbles reached Britain,

00:09:30.978 --> 00:09:34.595
Elgin had spent more than £70,000 on

00:09:34.597 --> 00:09:37.170
the operation, which is the equivalent

00:09:37.179 --> 00:09:40.301
of around £6 million in today's money.

00:09:40.987 --> 00:09:42.067
He had borrowed much of

00:09:42.068 --> 00:09:43.933
it, at high interest rates.

00:09:45.147 --> 00:09:48.519
Financially, he was ruined, and he was

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.836
forced to sell the sculptures to the

00:09:50.836 --> 00:09:53.440
British government, which paid him around

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:55.772
half of what he claimed to have spent.

00:09:56.579 --> 00:09:58.176
In turn, the government

00:09:58.362 --> 00:09:59.744
gave them to the museum.

00:10:01.062 --> 00:10:03.830
And then there are the Benin Bronzes.

00:10:04.657 --> 00:10:08.537
In 1897, a British force invaded

00:10:08.552 --> 00:10:10.880
the Kingdom of Benin, in what is

00:10:10.886 --> 00:10:13.539
now Nigeria, as revenge for the

00:10:13.555 --> 00:10:15.356
killing of a British delegation.

00:10:16.322 --> 00:10:18.736
They burned Benin City to the ground,

00:10:19.037 --> 00:10:22.358
sent the king into exile, and carried away

00:10:22.615 --> 00:10:25.560
thousands of extraordinary brass plaques

00:10:25.754 --> 00:10:28.102
and sculptures from the royal palace.

00:10:29.397 --> 00:10:31.251
The British government then sold

00:10:31.267 --> 00:10:33.435
much of this artwork to cover the

00:10:33.446 --> 00:10:35.169
costs of the military expedition.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.198
Several hundred pieces went to the

00:10:38.202 --> 00:10:40.884
British Museum, which today holds

00:10:40.884 --> 00:10:43.817
more than 900 objects from Benin.

00:10:45.172 --> 00:10:47.562
Here, nobody even claimed

00:10:47.595 --> 00:10:48.323
to have permission.

00:10:48.866 --> 00:10:51.311
The bronzes were simply taken.

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:53.295
Stolen, you might say.

00:10:54.623 --> 00:10:56.300
Plenty of the collection, however,

00:10:56.598 --> 00:10:59.353
arrived in other, friendlier, ways.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:02.775
Some of it was dug up with the agreement

00:11:02.775 --> 00:11:04.322
of the authorities of the time.

00:11:05.508 --> 00:11:08.589
In the 1840s, for example, a young

00:11:08.652 --> 00:11:11.213
adventurer named Austen Henry Layard

00:11:11.583 --> 00:11:13.956
dug into the mounds of northern Iraq.

00:11:14.668 --> 00:11:17.108
There, he uncovered the palaces

00:11:17.259 --> 00:11:19.826
of ancient Assyria, a civilisation

00:11:20.063 --> 00:11:21.872
then known mainly from the Bible.

00:11:23.129 --> 00:11:25.604
His finds included huge

00:11:25.703 --> 00:11:27.179
winged bulls of stone.

00:11:27.768 --> 00:11:29.576
They were floated down the River Tigris

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:32.769
on rafts made of inflated animal skins,

00:11:33.017 --> 00:11:35.602
and they caused a sensation in London.

00:11:36.628 --> 00:11:39.174
Ordinary Victorians queued to see a

00:11:39.181 --> 00:11:42.003
world nearly three thousand years old.

00:11:43.004 --> 00:11:46.000
And some of the most important finds were

00:11:46.002 --> 00:11:49.089
simply given, donated, to the museum.

00:11:50.432 --> 00:11:54.464
In 1938, a widow named Edith Pretty

00:11:54.825 --> 00:11:57.291
invited an archaeologist to dig

00:11:57.296 --> 00:11:59.450
up some mysterious mounds of earth

00:11:59.773 --> 00:12:01.754
on her land at Sutton Hoo, in

00:12:01.982 --> 00:12:03.877
Suffolk, in the east of England.

00:12:05.271 --> 00:12:08.064
The following year, under the largest one,

00:12:08.547 --> 00:12:11.528
he found the outline of an entire ship,

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.331
whose wood had long since rotted away.

00:12:15.395 --> 00:12:17.806
Inside it was the treasure of an

00:12:17.874 --> 00:12:21.471
Anglo-Saxon king: a warrior's helmet, gold

00:12:21.497 --> 00:12:24.809
buckles, and silver bowls from Byzantium.

00:12:25.852 --> 00:12:28.442
It was one of the greatest archaeological

00:12:28.442 --> 00:12:31.020
discoveries ever made in Britain, an

00:12:31.072 --> 00:12:34.263
invaluable collection, and it was ruled

00:12:34.495 --> 00:12:38.000
that all of it belonged to Mrs Pretty.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:41.032
Mrs Pretty gave it to

00:12:41.033 --> 00:12:42.911
the museum, every piece.

00:12:43.351 --> 00:12:45.903
She asked for nothing in return.

00:12:47.088 --> 00:12:48.620
At the time, it was the most

00:12:48.647 --> 00:12:51.281
valuable gift the museum had ever

00:12:51.282 --> 00:12:53.113
received from a living person.

00:12:54.149 --> 00:12:56.569
Now, we will come back to some of the

00:12:56.589 --> 00:12:58.901
arguments over whether the museum should

00:12:58.955 --> 00:13:01.184
actually be the custodian of this vast

00:13:01.207 --> 00:13:03.067
collection, or at least parts of it.

00:13:03.578 --> 00:13:05.593
But first, there is another part

00:13:05.611 --> 00:13:08.287
of the museum's story: its library.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:13.059
In 1857, a magnificent circular

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:14.634
reading room opened in the

00:13:14.638 --> 00:13:16.291
museum's central courtyard.

00:13:17.169 --> 00:13:20.550
It had a blue and gold dome, wider

00:13:20.735 --> 00:13:22.405
than that of St Paul's Cathedral.

00:13:23.645 --> 00:13:26.003
Beneath it sat hundreds of

00:13:26.025 --> 00:13:28.450
leather-topped desks, arranged in

00:13:28.451 --> 00:13:30.653
rows like the spokes of a wheel.

00:13:31.654 --> 00:13:34.332
And around it, on 40 kilometres

00:13:34.349 --> 00:13:36.206
of shelves, was one of the

00:13:36.207 --> 00:13:38.254
great libraries of the world.

00:13:39.282 --> 00:13:42.136
It was free to use, and you did

00:13:42.137 --> 00:13:44.645
not need to be rich or famous, just

00:13:44.654 --> 00:13:46.327
to have a letter of recommendation

00:13:46.652 --> 00:13:48.218
and a reason for study.

00:13:49.578 --> 00:13:52.312
Readers over the years included some

00:13:52.312 --> 00:13:54.969
of the greatest writers, thinkers, and

00:13:54.972 --> 00:13:57.838
politicians in modern history: Charles

00:13:57.839 --> 00:14:00.626
Dickens, Bram Stoker, Virginia Woolf,

00:14:00.816 --> 00:14:03.514
Mahatma Gandhi, and Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:14:04.341 --> 00:14:07.917
Even Karl Marx sat under that dome, day

00:14:07.957 --> 00:14:11.026
after day, for decades, researching and

00:14:11.028 --> 00:14:13.560
writing the book that became Das Kapital.

00:14:14.406 --> 00:14:16.822
And a few decades later, a Russian

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.186
revolutionary applied for a reader's

00:14:19.194 --> 00:14:21.938
ticket under the false name of Jacob

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:25.912
Richter; his real name was Vladimir Lenin.

00:14:26.903 --> 00:14:30.075
So, Karl Marx and Vladimir Lenin might

00:14:30.106 --> 00:14:31.694
not be the first people who come to

00:14:31.694 --> 00:14:34.295
mind as beneficiaries of the British

00:14:34.296 --> 00:14:37.449
state, but they owed some small debt

00:14:37.459 --> 00:14:39.638
of gratitude to the British Museum.

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:42.529
Now, if you're wondering where all

00:14:42.559 --> 00:14:45.483
these books are now, the library moved

00:14:45.514 --> 00:14:49.355
out in 1997, to the new British Library

00:14:49.356 --> 00:14:51.245
building a little bit up the road.

00:14:51.782 --> 00:14:53.711
The space around the reading room was

00:14:53.715 --> 00:14:56.097
transformed into the glass-roofed Great

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.562
Court that visitors walk through today.

00:14:59.557 --> 00:15:02.346
So, back to the objects, and

00:15:02.543 --> 00:15:04.058
to the arguments over them.

00:15:05.188 --> 00:15:08.026
As you may know, today, many of the

00:15:08.047 --> 00:15:10.719
countries they came from want them back.

00:15:11.609 --> 00:15:13.755
Greece has formally demanded

00:15:13.755 --> 00:15:15.456
the return of the Parthenon

00:15:15.471 --> 00:15:18.205
Marbles since the 1980s, when the

00:15:18.423 --> 00:15:21.018
actress-turned-culture-minister Melina

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:23.571
Mercouri made them a national cause.

00:15:24.719 --> 00:15:28.182
In 2009, Greece opened a gleaming

00:15:28.182 --> 00:15:30.767
museum at the foot of the Acropolis,

00:15:31.023 --> 00:15:33.069
with a top floor built to the

00:15:33.069 --> 00:15:35.402
exact dimensions of the Parthenon.

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:38.961
The pieces that are in London are shown

00:15:38.961 --> 00:15:42.309
there as plain white plaster copies, so

00:15:42.318 --> 00:15:45.144
visitors can see exactly what is missing.

00:15:46.566 --> 00:15:48.588
Greece is far from alone.

00:15:49.061 --> 00:15:51.516
Nigeria wants the Benin Bronzes.

00:15:51.751 --> 00:15:53.597
Egyptian archaeologists have

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.862
campaigned for the Rosetta Stone.

00:15:56.933 --> 00:15:59.990
And on Easter Island, or Rapa Nui,

00:16:00.261 --> 00:16:02.593
to give it its Polynesian name, the

00:16:02.593 --> 00:16:04.847
islanders want back a giant stone

00:16:04.849 --> 00:16:07.435
figure taken by a British warship in

00:16:07.491 --> 00:16:10.603
1868 and presented to Queen Victoria.

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:13.720
The islanders' name for it is

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:16.026
usually translated as "the lost

00:16:16.036 --> 00:16:18.309
friend" or "the stolen friend".

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:21.602
The museum's traditional defence

00:16:21.852 --> 00:16:24.087
rests on three arguments.

00:16:24.598 --> 00:16:28.041
First, a legal one: an Act of

00:16:28.041 --> 00:16:32.025
Parliament from 1963 actually forbids

00:16:32.202 --> 00:16:34.548
the museum from giving away almost

00:16:34.611 --> 00:16:36.902
anything in its collection, so the

00:16:36.916 --> 00:16:39.445
trustees say their hands are tied.

00:16:39.922 --> 00:16:41.331
Legally, they aren't allowed

00:16:41.349 --> 00:16:42.608
to give anything back.

00:16:43.843 --> 00:16:47.540
Second, a philosophical one: that there is

00:16:47.541 --> 00:16:51.317
real value in a universal museum, a single

00:16:51.318 --> 00:16:54.489
place where a visitor can walk from Egypt

00:16:54.632 --> 00:16:58.267
to Greece to Benin in an afternoon and see

00:16:58.471 --> 00:17:01.758
human civilisation as one connected story.

00:17:03.032 --> 00:17:05.148
And, after all, the museum

00:17:05.148 --> 00:17:06.720
is still free to all.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:09.039
Granted, you need to get there,

00:17:09.092 --> 00:17:11.804
to get to London, but when you do,

00:17:12.142 --> 00:17:14.129
the collection is there for you.

00:17:15.657 --> 00:17:18.701
And third, a defence of security.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.664
Some of these ancient treasures,

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:23.934
its defenders say, simply wouldn't

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:26.019
be adequately protected if they

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:28.299
were returned to areas of conflict.

00:17:28.861 --> 00:17:30.974
Just take a look at what ISIS did to

00:17:30.975 --> 00:17:33.754
sites of cultural value in Syria and Iraq.

00:17:34.423 --> 00:17:37.336
In 2015, it destroyed much of

00:17:37.339 --> 00:17:39.318
Nimrud, the very place where

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:41.949
Layard had dug up his winged bulls.

00:17:43.305 --> 00:17:45.329
The museum has held to this

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.429
defence for decades, but in recent

00:17:48.441 --> 00:17:51.548
years, things seem to be shifting.

00:17:52.522 --> 00:17:53.775
Other countries have

00:17:53.776 --> 00:17:55.162
started to give things back.

00:17:56.026 --> 00:17:58.855
Germany has signed over ownership of

00:17:58.855 --> 00:18:00.884
more than a thousand Benin Bronzes

00:18:01.091 --> 00:18:03.553
to Nigeria, and the Netherlands

00:18:03.718 --> 00:18:05.741
has sent back more than a hundred.

00:18:06.849 --> 00:18:08.859
And the British Museum and Greece

00:18:09.068 --> 00:18:11.257
have spent several years talking

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:12.773
about an arrangement in which

00:18:12.773 --> 00:18:15.645
sculptures could return to Athens

00:18:15.879 --> 00:18:18.329
as part of a long-term exchange.

00:18:19.037 --> 00:18:21.326
More than once, reports in the press

00:18:21.584 --> 00:18:23.707
have suggested a deal was close.

00:18:24.578 --> 00:18:27.261
But the talks keep running into

00:18:27.261 --> 00:18:29.185
the same problem, which is the

00:18:29.185 --> 00:18:31.333
question of who owns the sculptures.

00:18:32.021 --> 00:18:35.247
The museum says it can only lend them.

00:18:35.855 --> 00:18:38.939
Greece says it cannot borrow something

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.323
that it believes already belongs to it.

00:18:42.837 --> 00:18:45.632
And then there is the question of how well

00:18:45.637 --> 00:18:48.800
the museum looks after what it holds, and

00:18:48.861 --> 00:18:51.096
here the defence of "we're keeping things

00:18:51.096 --> 00:18:54.182
safe" starts to look a little shaky.

00:18:55.513 --> 00:18:59.445
In 1845, a drunken visitor picked up

00:18:59.477 --> 00:19:02.470
a lump of stone and hurled it at the

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:05.115
Portland Vase, a piece of Roman glass

00:19:05.345 --> 00:19:08.314
nearly 2,000 years old and possibly

00:19:08.546 --> 00:19:10.610
the most famous vase in the world.

00:19:11.599 --> 00:19:15.555
It shattered into more than 180 fragments.

00:19:16.541 --> 00:19:18.486
The museum glued it back together.

00:19:19.198 --> 00:19:21.464
And has since taken it apart

00:19:21.464 --> 00:19:23.926
and restored it again, twice, as

00:19:23.926 --> 00:19:25.618
restoration techniques improved.

00:19:26.356 --> 00:19:28.201
Today, you can hardly see the

00:19:28.201 --> 00:19:30.746
joins, but still, it was damaged

00:19:30.923 --> 00:19:32.669
under the British Museum's watch.

00:19:33.824 --> 00:19:37.115
But in 2023, the museum

00:19:37.326 --> 00:19:39.317
faced a much bigger scandal.

00:19:40.371 --> 00:19:43.891
It emerged that around 2,000 objects

00:19:44.141 --> 00:19:45.936
had gone missing or been damaged

00:19:46.144 --> 00:19:47.940
over a period of several years.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:52.302
Most were small gems, jewellery

00:19:52.303 --> 00:19:54.246
and fragments from the storerooms.

00:19:54.931 --> 00:19:57.588
The museum alleges that they had been

00:19:57.591 --> 00:20:00.798
secretly removed by one of its own senior

00:20:00.814 --> 00:20:03.210
curators, with some pieces offered for

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:05.390
sale for a fraction of their value.

00:20:06.628 --> 00:20:08.848
One Roman object, reportedly

00:20:09.016 --> 00:20:11.836
worth as much as £50,000, was

00:20:11.846 --> 00:20:14.309
offered for sale for just £40.

00:20:15.142 --> 00:20:17.549
Some had been bought by collectors who

00:20:17.551 --> 00:20:19.083
had no idea what they were holding.

00:20:20.195 --> 00:20:22.582
And the museum had been warned.

00:20:23.402 --> 00:20:26.327
An antiques dealer in Denmark had noticed

00:20:26.337 --> 00:20:29.423
suspicious pieces appearing on eBay and

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:32.717
had emailed the museum about it in 2021,

00:20:33.123 --> 00:20:35.395
two years before the scandal broke.

00:20:36.323 --> 00:20:39.102
He was told, in effect, that he was wrong,

00:20:39.327 --> 00:20:41.355
and that there was nothing to worry about.

00:20:42.661 --> 00:20:45.041
When the scale of the losses finally

00:20:45.041 --> 00:20:48.137
became clear in the summer of 2023,

00:20:48.533 --> 00:20:50.443
the story went around the world.

00:20:50.964 --> 00:20:52.671
The director resigned.

00:20:52.924 --> 00:20:55.947
The curator was dismissed, and later

00:20:55.957 --> 00:20:58.662
ordered by a court to hand over his eBay

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.677
records and to return what he still held.

00:21:03.013 --> 00:21:05.076
Museum staff began the long

00:21:05.081 --> 00:21:06.391
work of hunting their own

00:21:06.415 --> 00:21:08.008
collection across the internet.

00:21:08.669 --> 00:21:10.358
Hundreds of pieces have

00:21:10.358 --> 00:21:11.894
since been recovered, but

00:21:11.916 --> 00:21:13.808
many more are still missing.

00:21:15.141 --> 00:21:16.940
Part of the problem, it turned

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:19.331
out, was that the museum did

00:21:19.333 --> 00:21:21.456
not fully know what it owned.

00:21:21.843 --> 00:21:23.980
Millions of objects had never

00:21:23.982 --> 00:21:25.704
been photographed or properly

00:21:25.712 --> 00:21:28.466
catalogued, so when small things

00:21:28.477 --> 00:21:30.460
vanished from a storeroom, their

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:32.585
disappearance could go unnoticed.

00:21:33.451 --> 00:21:35.567
The museum has since promised to

00:21:35.567 --> 00:21:38.189
photograph and catalogue the entire

00:21:38.201 --> 00:21:41.131
collection, but this is a huge project

00:21:41.691 --> 00:21:43.938
that is expected to take years.

00:21:44.765 --> 00:21:46.652
So, where does that

00:21:46.665 --> 00:21:48.454
leave the British Museum?

00:21:49.234 --> 00:21:51.740
It is one of the most argued-about museums

00:21:51.969 --> 00:21:54.491
in the world, and still one of the most

00:21:54.493 --> 00:21:57.426
visited, with around six million people

00:21:57.575 --> 00:21:59.721
walking through its gates every year.

00:22:00.742 --> 00:22:04.228
It is, to some people, a crime scene, a

00:22:04.228 --> 00:22:07.289
warehouse full of stolen goods waiting

00:22:07.296 --> 00:22:09.726
to be returned to their rightful owners.

00:22:10.657 --> 00:22:12.759
To others, it is a marvellous

00:22:12.796 --> 00:22:14.541
collection of human ingenuity,

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:17.565
creativity, and progress through time.

00:22:18.365 --> 00:22:20.856
Whatever you may think of it, there

00:22:20.856 --> 00:22:23.253
is one thing that has not changed in

00:22:23.253 --> 00:22:26.288
the almost 300 years of its existence.

00:22:26.944 --> 00:22:28.237
It is free.

00:22:28.604 --> 00:22:31.347
Anyone, old or young, rich or

00:22:31.347 --> 00:22:34.175
poor, can walk in and see some

00:22:34.194 --> 00:22:36.086
of the most remarkable things

00:22:36.272 --> 00:22:38.347
the world has ever produced.

00:22:38.927 --> 00:22:41.432
And I, for one, think that

00:22:41.541 --> 00:22:43.236
is something pretty special.

00:22:44.512 --> 00:22:46.470
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:22:46.487 --> 00:22:48.726
episode on the British Museum.

00:22:48.984 --> 00:22:50.383
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:22:50.643 --> 00:22:52.363
and that you've learnt something new.

00:22:52.966 --> 00:22:54.774
You've been listening to English Learning

00:22:54.775 --> 00:22:57.492
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:00.884
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:03.499
I'll catch you in the next episode.

